# Scarcity in Low-Level Design

> Managing limited resources when demand exceeds supply.

The constraint is **capacity**, not correctness. You have N threads competing for M resources where M < N. The job is to coordinate the waiting without the system falling over.

---

## TL;DR Decision Tree

```
Is the resource a stateful object you must hand out (DB connection, GPU)?
├── YES → Blocking Queue (resource pool)
└── NO  → Semaphore
          ├── Each op consumes 1 unit?         → Semaphore with N permits
          └── Each op consumes variable units? → Semaphore where 1 permit = 1 unit
```

If utilization (not governance) is the goal → **work stealing**, **batching**, or **adaptive sizing**.

---

## Two Core Solutions

### 1. Semaphore — limit *how many* can do something at once

A counter with `acquire()` / `release()`. Threads block when the counter hits zero, OS wakes them on release. Use it when there is **no actual object to hand out**, just permission.

**Analogy:** Nightclub with 100 tokens. Bouncer hands one out per entry, takes one back per exit.

```cpp
#include <semaphore>
#include <string>

class APIClient {
private:
    std::counting_semaphore<5> requestPermits{5};
    HttpClient httpClient;

public:
    Response makeRequest(const std::string& endpoint) {
        requestPermits.acquire();
        try {
            auto response = httpClient.get(endpoint);
            requestPermits.release();
            return response;
        } catch (...) {
            requestPermits.release();
            throw;
        }
    }
};
```

**Critical rule:** Always release in a `catch` (or RAII guard). Forgetting this is the #1 bug interviewers probe.

#### RAII helper (preferred in real code)

```cpp
template <std::ptrdiff_t N>
class SemaphoreGuard {
    std::counting_semaphore<N>& sem;
public:
    explicit SemaphoreGuard(std::counting_semaphore<N>& s) : sem(s) { sem.acquire(); }
    ~SemaphoreGuard() { sem.release(); }
    SemaphoreGuard(const SemaphoreGuard&) = delete;
    SemaphoreGuard& operator=(const SemaphoreGuard&) = delete;
};
```

