### 2. Resource Pool (Blocking Queue) — hand out *the actual object*

When the resource is a stateful, expensive-to-create object (open socket, GPU context, file handle), you need to dispense and reclaim instances. C++ has no built-in blocking queue, so you build one with `std::queue` + `std::mutex` + `std::condition_variable`.

```cpp
#include <queue>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <chrono>
#include <stdexcept>

class ConnectionPool {
private:
    std::queue<Connection*> available;
    std::mutex mtx;
    std::condition_variable cv;
    std::chrono::milliseconds timeout;

public:
    ConnectionPool(int poolSize, std::chrono::milliseconds t)
        : timeout(t) {
        for (int i = 0; i < poolSize; ++i) {
            available.push(createNewConnection());
        }
    }

    Connection* acquire() {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
        if (!cv.wait_for(lock, timeout, [this] { return !available.empty(); })) {
            throw std::runtime_error("No connection available within timeout");
        }
        Connection* conn = available.front();
        available.pop();
        return conn;
    }

    void release(Connection* conn) {
        {
            std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
            available.push(conn);
        }
        cv.notify_one();
    }

    void executeQuery(const std::string& query) {
        Connection* conn = acquire();
        try {
            conn->execute(query);
            release(conn);
        } catch (...) {
            release(conn);
            throw;
        }
    }
};
```

**Why a pool, not just a semaphore?** A semaphore caps concurrency but doesn't store the actual `Connection*`. The queue both bounds concurrency *and* dispenses objects.

