## The Four Patterns You'll See in Interviews

### Pattern 1 — Limit Concurrent Operations

> "Limit to N concurrent X." No object to hand out.

Examples: download manager, rate-limited API client, image/video transcoder.

```cpp
#include <semaphore>
#include <fstream>

class DownloadManager {
private:
    std::counting_semaphore<3> slots{3};
    HttpClient httpClient;

public:
    void download(const std::string& url, const std::string& dest) {
        slots.acquire();
        try {
            auto data = httpClient.download(url);
            std::ofstream file(dest, std::ios::binary);
            file.write(reinterpret_cast<const char*>(data.data()), data.size());
            slots.release();
        } catch (...) {
            slots.release();
            throw;
        }
    }
};
```

---

### Pattern 2 — Limit Aggregate Consumption

> Operations consume *variable amounts* of a shared budget (bandwidth, memory, I/O).

1 permit = 1 unit of resource. Threads acquire permits proportional to what they'll consume.

```cpp
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <fstream>
#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>

class DiskWriter {
private:
    static constexpr int MB = 1024 * 1024;
    std::mutex mtx;
    std::condition_variable cv;
    int available = 100; // 100 MB budget

public:
    void writeFile(const std::vector<char>& data, const std::string& path) {
        int permits = std::max(1, static_cast<int>((data.size() + MB - 1) / MB));

        {
            std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
            cv.wait(lock, [this, permits] { return available >= permits; });
            available -= permits;
        }

        try {
            std::ofstream file(path, std::ios::binary);
            file.write(data.data(), data.size());
        } catch (...) {
            {
                std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
                available += permits;
            }
            cv.notify_all();
            throw;
        }

        {
            std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
            available += permits;
        }
        cv.notify_all();
    }
};
```

> Note: this caps *concurrent bytes in flight*, not throughput rate. True rate limiting (100 MB/s) needs **token-bucket**-style time-based replenishment.

Examples: in-flight upload limiter, memory budget for buffers.

---

### Pattern 3 — Reuse Expensive Objects

> Stateful objects that are slow/expensive to create (DB connections, GPU contexts, file handles).

→ Use the **blocking queue / resource pool** from Solution 2.

Examples:
- DB connection pool (10 connections serving 1000 RPS).
- GPU task scheduler (4 GPUs with pre-loaded model weights).

---

### Pattern 4 — Maximize Utilization

When governance isn't enough — interviewer asks "how do you keep the GPUs/connections fully busy?"

| Technique | When to use | How |
|---|---|---|
| **Work stealing** | Task durations vary wildly | Per-worker queues; idle workers steal from busy ones (Java `ForkJoinPool`, Go scheduler) |
| **Batching** | Many small operations dominate pool overhead | Acquire once, do N units of work, release. Trades latency for throughput |
| **Adaptive sizing** | Demand fluctuates significantly | Grow pool under load, shrink when idle (HikariCP, pgbouncer). Tune carefully or you thrash |

---

## Predictable Failure Modes

- **Permit leak** — missing `release` on the exception path → pool drains forever.
- **Unbounded queue** — no capacity argument → OOM.
- **Indefinite blocking** — `wait()` instead of `wait_for()` on a request path → users see hangs, threads pile up.
- **Check-then-act without locks** — TOCTOU lets threads exceed the limit.
- **Stale resources** — connection died during idle; handing it out fails the next request.

---

## Interview Cheat-Sheet Phrases

- "I'll use a semaphore with N permits to cap concurrent operations."
- "Connections are stateful objects, so I'll use a `BlockingQueue` of `Connection*` — threads `acquire`, use, and `release` in a catch block."
- "I'll use `wait_for` with a timeout instead of `wait` so request paths fail fast instead of blocking forever."
- "I'll create connections upfront in the constructor — simpler, no lazy-init race conditions, predictable runtime."
- "Each operation consumes a variable amount of bandwidth, so 1 permit = 1 MB. Operations acquire permits equal to their size."
- "To maximize utilization with uneven task lengths, I'd add per-worker queues with work stealing."

---

## One-Line Summary

> **Scarcity = bounded capacity + blocking coordination.** Pick a semaphore for permission, a blocking queue for objects, add timeouts on request paths, always release in a catch block.
